---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Demo from '../components/Demo.astro';
import { DOCS } from '../lib/site';
---

<Base title="How GCDS works" current="how-it-works">
  <gcds-text>
    Before you install anything, it helps to know what you’re installing. This section covers the questions
    developers ask most: what web components are, what shadow DOM does, how much you can customize, and
    whether it’s really accessible. It also covers the parts that aren’t perfect yet.
  </gcds-text>

  <gcds-heading tag="h2">GCDS components are just HTML tags</gcds-heading>
  <gcds-text>
    GCDS is built with <strong>web components</strong>, a browser standard, not a framework. A web component is
    a custom HTML tag that the browser knows how to draw. Once the GCDS script is on the page, you write one tag and get a working, accessible, on-brand button:
  </gcds-text>
  <Demo html={`<gcds-button type="button">Continue</gcds-button>`} />
  <gcds-text>
    The code under the example changes to match the framework you chose. Use the menu above the example to
    compare frameworks.
  </gcds-text>
  <gcds-text>
    Because the browser itself understands these tags, the same component works in plain HTML, React, Vue,
    Angular, a CMS template or a server-rendered Java or .NET app. The framework packages
    (<code>@gcds-core/components-react</code> and so on) are thin wrappers that make the tags feel native in each
    framework: typed props, <code>v-model</code>, <code>ngModel</code>, and so on.
  </gcds-text>

  <gcds-heading tag="h2">What shadow DOM is, and why GCDS uses it</gcds-heading>
  <gcds-text>
    Inside each GCDS component is its own small piece of HTML and CSS, called a <strong>shadow root</strong>.
    Styles inside it don’t leak out, and, more importantly, styles from your page don’t leak in.
  </gcds-text>

  <gcds-details details-title="Try it: look inside a component">
    <ol class="list-decimal">
      <li><gcds-text margin-bottom="100">Right-click the “Continue” button above and choose <strong>Inspect</strong>.</gcds-text></li>
      <li><gcds-text margin-bottom="100">In the Elements panel, expand <code>&lt;gcds-button&gt;</code>. You’ll see <code>#shadow-root (open)</code>.</gcds-text></li>
      <li><gcds-text margin-bottom="100">Expand it. Inside is an ordinary <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> with ordinary CSS. Nothing is hidden from you; the shadow root is open and fully inspectable.</gcds-text></li>
    </ol>
  </gcds-details>

  <gcds-heading tag="h3">Why that protection matters</gcds-heading>
  <gcds-text>
    Government sites are rarely built by one team. A button may end up on a page with a decade-old theme, a CMS
    stylesheet, and a third-party widget, all with their own CSS. Press the button below to apply some
    aggressive page CSS to this example and see what happens to each button.
  </gcds-text>

  <Demo
    id="leak-demo"
    html={`<gcds-grid columns="max-content max-content" gap="500" align-items="start">
  <div>
    <gcds-text size="small" margin-bottom="100">Plain HTML button</gcds-text>
    <button type="button">Continue</button>
  </div>
  <div>
    <gcds-text size="small" margin-bottom="100">GCDS button</gcds-text>
    <gcds-button type="button">Continue</gcds-button>
  </div>
</gcds-grid>`}
  />
  <gcds-button type="button" button-role="secondary" id="leak-toggle">Apply aggressive page CSS</gcds-button>
  <gcds-text role="status" id="leak-status" margin-top="200"></gcds-text>
  <gcds-text>This is the CSS the button applies to the example:</gcds-text>
  <CodeBlock
    lang="css"
    code={`#leak-demo.hostile button {
  background: yellow;
  color: yellow;          /* yellow on yellow: unreadable */
  font-size: 0.6rem;
  outline: none;          /* removes the keyboard focus ring */
}`}
  />
  <gcds-text>
    The plain button breaks: unreadable text, tiny type, and no focus ring for keyboard users. The GCDS button
    keeps its colours, size and focus style, because the page CSS can’t reach inside its shadow root. That’s the
    trade: you give up the ability to restyle a component’s internals, and in return every GCDS component stays
    readable, accessible and on brand on <em>any</em> page.
  </gcds-text>

  <gcds-heading tag="h2">“It’s not flexible enough”</gcds-heading>
  <gcds-text>
    This is the most common concern, so here’s a direct answer. GCDS is deliberately flexible about
    <strong>content and layout</strong> and deliberately strict about <strong>brand and accessibility</strong>.
    When a request to “just change the button” comes up, it’s usually asking for one of the strict things:
    a non-GC colour, a smaller tap target, a different font.
  </gcds-text>

  <gcds-heading tag="h3">What you can change</gcds-heading>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">You want to…</th><th scope="col">Use</th><th scope="col">Example</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Change a component’s variant, size or behaviour</td><td>Attributes (props)</td><td><code>button-role="secondary"</code>, <code>size="small"</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Put your own content inside a component</td><td>Slots</td><td>Text, links and other components inside <code>&lt;gcds-notice&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;gcds-details&gt;</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Lay out components on the page</td><td>Grid, container, CSS Shortcuts</td><td><code>&lt;gcds-grid columns-desktop="1fr 1fr"&gt;</code>, <code>class="mb-400"</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Style your own markup to match</td><td>Design tokens</td><td><code>color: var(--gcds-text-primary)</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Respond to interactions</td><td>Events</td><td><code>gcdsClick</code>, <code>gcdsChange</code>, <code>gcdsValid</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Use components in forms</td><td>Native form participation</td><td>Inputs submit with a regular <code>&lt;form&gt;</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <gcds-heading tag="h3">What you shouldn’t change, and what to do instead</gcds-heading>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Common request</th><th scope="col">Why it’s restricted</th><th scope="col">Do this instead</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>“Make the button our program colour”</td><td>GC brand colours are tested for contrast and recognized as official government</td><td>Use <code>button-role</code> (primary, secondary, danger, start). Bring program identity into content and imagery, not controls.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>“Make it smaller to fit”</td><td>Target sizes meet accessibility requirements</td><td>Use <code>size="small"</code> where it exists, or rethink the layout with grid and container.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>“Use our own font”</td><td>Lato and Noto Sans are the GC typefaces</td><td>Keep the GC fonts; use heading levels and text roles for hierarchy.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>“Remove the focus outline, it looks odd”</td><td>Keyboard users need to see where they are</td><td>Leave it. It’s required for WCAG 2.1 AA.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>“I need a component GCDS doesn’t have”</td><td>—</td><td>Build it from tokens and CSS Shortcuts, then <gcds-link href={`${DOCS}/contact/`}>tell the GCDS team</gcds-link>. Requests shape the roadmap.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <gcds-heading tag="h2">“Is it really accessible?”</gcds-heading>
  <gcds-text>
    Shadow DOM doesn’t hide anything from assistive technology. Screen readers read the rendered page, shadow
    content included. What matters is whether the component inside is built correctly, and that’s where GCDS
    does the work for you:
  </gcds-text>
  <ul class="list-disc mb-400">
    <li><gcds-text margin-bottom="100">Components meet or exceed <strong>WCAG 2.1 AA</strong>, and are tested with automated tools and by people with disabilities. See <gcds-link href={`${DOCS}/accessibility/`}>accessibility in GCDS</gcds-link>.</gcds-text></li>
    <li><gcds-text margin-bottom="100">Components are covered by automated <strong>axe</strong> accessibility tests that run on every change to the code.</gcds-text></li>
    <li><gcds-text margin-bottom="100">Form components pass keyboard focus straight to the real input inside, and take part in native <code>&lt;form&gt;</code> submission and validation.</gcds-text></li>
    <li><gcds-text margin-bottom="100">Labels, hints, error messages and their ARIA connections are built in, in English and French.</gcds-text></li>
  </ul>
  <gcds-text>
    You’re still responsible for the page around the components: heading order, meaningful link text, plain
    language. GCDS gets the hard, repetitive parts right so you can focus on those.
  </gcds-text>

  <gcds-details details-title="Try it with your keyboard">
    <gcds-text>Press "<kbd>Tab</kbd>" to move into this field, type something, then press "<kbd>Tab</kbd>" again to leave it empty and trigger validation.</gcds-text>
    <gcds-input input-id="a11y-demo" name="a11y-demo" label="Your department" hint="For example: Employment and Social Development Canada" required></gcds-input>
    <gcds-text>
      Leave it empty and the error message appears and is announced by screen readers. You didn’t have to write
      any ARIA to get that.
    </gcds-text>
  </gcds-details>

  <gcds-heading tag="h2" id="javascript">“Doesn’t it need JavaScript?”</gcds-heading>
  <gcds-text>
    Yes, and so does almost everything else on the web. JavaScript runs on
    <gcds-link href="https://w3techs.com/technologies/details/cp-javascript" external>98.9% of websites</gcds-link>.
    Menus, search, form validation, analytics and most third-party tools already depend on it. For most sites, adding GCDS doesn’t change what you require of your visitors.
  </gcds-text>
  <gcds-text>
    Very few people switch JavaScript off. When GOV.UK
    <gcds-link href="https://gds.blog.gov.uk/2013/10/21/how-many-people-are-missing-out-on-javascript-enhancement/" external>measured it</gcds-link>,
    1.1% of visits didn’t get JavaScript, but only 0.2% had it disabled or unsupported. For most of the rest,
    JavaScript was turned on but didn’t run, for example because of a dropped connection, a blocked script or an
    error.
  </gcds-text>
  <gcds-text>
    So the useful question isn’t “what if users turn it off?” but “what if it fails?” For critical services, test
    it: block the GCDS script in your browser’s developer tools and check what people can still do.
  </gcds-text>

  <gcds-heading tag="h2">The honest part: current limitations</gcds-heading>
  <gcds-notice notice-role="warning" notice-title="Know these before you start" notice-title-tag="h3">
    <ul class="list-disc">
      <li><gcds-text margin-bottom="100"><strong>Components need JavaScript.</strong> If it doesn’t load, people see the plain text you put inside the tags, but not the styling or anything set by attributes, like a card’s title. See <gcds-link href="#javascript">“Doesn’t it need JavaScript?”</gcds-link> above.</gcds-text></li>
      <li><gcds-text margin-bottom="100"><strong>Server-side rendering isn’t supported yet.</strong> Components render in the browser. SSR support is on the <gcds-link href={`${DOCS}/roadmap/`}>GCDS roadmap</gcds-link>.</gcds-text></li>
      <li><gcds-text margin-bottom="100"><strong>Styling hooks are intentionally few.</strong> See the flexibility section above. This is a choice, not an oversight.</gcds-text></li>
      <li><gcds-text margin-bottom="0"><strong>The component set is still growing.</strong> If you need something that isn’t there, build it from tokens and let the team know.</gcds-text></li>
    </ul>
  </gcds-notice>
</Base>

<style>
  /* Reaches the plain button in the example's light DOM, but not inside gcds-button's shadow root. */
  :global(#leak-demo.hostile button) {
    background: yellow;
    color: yellow;
    font-size: 0.6rem;
    outline: none;
  }
</style>

<script>
  const demo = document.getElementById('leak-demo')!;
  const toggle = document.getElementById('leak-toggle')!;
  const status = document.getElementById('leak-status')!;
  toggle.addEventListener('gcdsClick', () => {
    const on = demo.classList.toggle('hostile');
    toggle.textContent = on ? 'Remove aggressive page CSS' : 'Apply aggressive page CSS';
    status.textContent = on
      ? 'Aggressive CSS applied. The plain button is now unreadable; the GCDS button is unchanged.'
      : 'Aggressive CSS removed.';
  });
</script>
